<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aristotle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">384–<a href="/years/322-bce">322 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aristotle was a Greek philosopher of the fourth century BCE whose works covered logic, ethics, politics and natural science. His writings were translated into Arabic under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> and commented upon by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/averroes">Averroes</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a>, and they were preserved and studied in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. His Politics also contains a well-known discussion of the constitution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aristotle.jpg" alt="Aristotle" width="314" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Lysippos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aristotle_Altemps_Inv8575.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
